## 7. Key Considerations & Risks for Root Changes

If you plan to perform **root/architectural modifications**, keep the following key areas in mind:

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **1. API Architecture & Routing**
> Currently, every API endpoint is an individual `.php` file called via relative URL (e.g. `/api/auth/login.php`). If introducing a PHP framework (Slim/Laravel) or unified router, all endpoint URLs in `frontend/src/services/api.js` must be updated or mapped carefully via URL rewriting (`.htaccess` / Nginx).

> [!WARNING]
> **2. Hardcoded Skills Catalog**
> `SKILLS_CATALOG` is defined as a PHP constant in both `api/matches/eligible.php` and `api/ai/proxy.php`. If introducing dynamic skill management or new categories, convert `SKILLS_CATALOG` into a database table (`skills` catalog table) so both the AI proxy and matching engine query it dynamically.

> [!NOTE]
> **3. Session & Authentication Model**
> The app relies on PHP native sessions (`thinktank_session`) with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` and `X-CSRF-Token` headers. If shifting to a mobile app or stateless REST API, refactor authentication to use JWT / Bearer tokens.

> [!TIP]
> **4. Data Model Normalization**
> The `users` table contains `availability` and `enrolled_courses` as raw JSON columns, while `user_skills` and `project_skills` use separate junction tables. Ensure database migrations preserve JSON compatibility when refactoring user profiles.
